{
 "title": "Saturation (sublimation) pressures of the gases and ices: Fray and Schmitt 2009 polynomials, water by Feistel and Wagner",
 "basis": "The solid curve is below the triple point and the liquid curve above it, up to the critical point. Liquid: Wagner fits to the NIST saturation tables, P = Pc exp((a x + b x^1.5 + c x^2.5 + d x^5)/Tr), x = 1 - T/Tc, Tr = T/Tc, P in Pa (COMPUTED by the Atmosphere research lane, part 01 section 6.2, max error 0.002 to 0.26 percent against the table); hydrogen cyanide by the Perry DIPPR 101 correlation ln P[Pa] = A + B/T + C ln T + D T^E (259.83 to 456.65 K, DATA, part 01 section 7.4); acetylene has no liquid curve. Fray and Schmitt polynomials are ln P[bar] = A0 + sum_{i=1..6} Ai / T^i for the solid, with the coefficients as quoted in the supplementary Table 2 of Lisse et al. arXiv:2009.02277 (2ND-HAND, read from the table image; SO2 A1 and Kr A6 typos corrected, both confirmed only by the triple-point check). Water is ln(P/Ptr) = 1.5 ln(T/Ttr) + (1 - Ttr/T) sum ei theta^i, theta = T/Ttr, Ptr = 611.657 Pa, Ttr = 273.16 K. Where a species has more than one fit, `from_k` is where the second takes over: the temperature at which the two fits cross (the solid transitions of N2, CO, O2 and CH3OH), or at which they come closest when they do not cross (CO2). The windows of the original paper (Planet. Space Sci. 57, 2053) were not read, so every `from_k` and every `floor_k` is a STAND-IN derived from the fits themselves; `floor_k` is the temperature below which the polynomial turns downwards and means nothing.",
 "source": "docs/design/atmosphere-composition-parts/06-condensates-clouds-hazes-dust-minerals.md section 2.4 and 01-gas-table-thermodynamics.md section 7.2 (O2-3, Ne-2); docs/design/body-composition-research.md part F; Feistel and Wagner 2007, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 35, 1021 (water, as quoted)",
